# Dead-branch predication. The else-arm divides by zero, but the condition `a == a`
# is always true, so that arm is DEAD at runtime. The prover's circuit model builds
# BOTH arms of an `if` — yet this now PROVES the live result (`a`), because the
# division gadget's `r < b` check is predicated on an in-circuit `b != 0` bit: when
# the divisor is zero the check goes vacuous, so the dead `a % 0` no longer poisons
# the circuit, and the mux selects the live arm.
#
#   glass prove examples/prove/deadbranch_div.glass a=7 b=0     # -> result: 7, ACCEPT
#
# (v5.88 — predicated gadgets, the dead-branch paradigm shift. A LIVE divide-by-zero
#  is still refused: `glass prove "a % b" a=7 b=0` ABSTAINs, since seval short-circuits
#  to the taken path and catches it. Pure GMul+GEqZero predication — no verifier change.)

if a == a then a else a % b
